  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 AM EDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nUnsettled weather will liner through the remainder of the \nweekend as weak disturbances continue moving through broad \nnorthwest flow aloft. While there will be opportunities for \nshowers and thunderstorms each afternoon, limited moisture \nshould keep activity fairly scattered, leaving plenty of dry \ntime between any convection. \n\nA weak boundary sliding through northern Michigan this \nafternoon should provide enough focus for isolated to scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms mainly across eastern Upper with \nactivity chances expanding south across northern Lower through \ntonight, although still remaining scattered in nature. Overnight\nlows generally look to be warmer than last night with most \nlocations staying in the 60s and eastern Upper flirting in the \nupper 50s to low 60s. Another wave of energy crosses the area \nSunday, bringing another round of hit-or- miss showers and \nthunderstorms. Moisture will be increasing, but forcing looks to\nremain fairly weak, keeping chances pretty scattered and low \nthrough the afternoon/ early evening.\n\nBetter opportunity for showers and thunderstorms arrives late \nSunday through Monday as a stronger disturbance moves through \nthe region. Increased moisture and stronger forcing should \nsupport more widespread shower and thunderstorm development \ncompared to earlier in the weekend. While some uncertainty \nremains regarding the exact evolution of convection, the overall\nenvironment could support a few stronger storms late Sunday \nthrough Monday, with gusty to damaging winds appearing to be the\nprimary threat and locally heavy rainfall possible with a more \nmoisture-rich airmass in place... SPC and WPC continue to \nhighlight our region in a Marginal Risk for severe storms and \nexcessive rainfall potential. \n\nBehind this system, high pressure gradually builds back into \nthe Great Lakes through mid-next week with dry weather looking \nto settle back into northern Michigan Tuesday through at least \nThursday. Temperatures will continue to warm with highs climbing\ninto the 80s once again after a slight \"cooling\" early in the \nweek.",
